gitignore/
node_modules/
firebase-debug.log
.parcel-cache/
.firebase/
/venv/

# ===== Android Studio / Gradle =====
/android/.gradle/
/android/build/
/android/local.properties

# Ignore runtime "save" folder inside android
/android/save/

# Ignore generated IDE files
/android/*.iml
/android/.idea/
/android/captures/
/android/.externalNativeBuild/

# Ignore logs
/android/*.log

# OS-specific files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Optional: ignore native build outputs
/android/.cxx/
/android/lint/intermediates/
/android/lint/generated/

/scripts/temp/

# A history rewrite clones the repo bare and works on the clone. Four of those
# clones were done inside this working tree, and every commit here sweeps the
# whole tree — so all four landed in the repo. Their packfiles are already
# compressed, so git cannot shrink them again: 106.9 MiB of the 360 MiB pack is
# four copies of an attempt to make the pack smaller. Do the rewrite outside the
# working tree; this line is only the second net.
*-clean-*.git/

# Bundler output. Nothing tracks it now, but 6.8 MiB of history is one stray
# source map from when something did.
/dist/

# An installer downloaded into the tree and swept in by a commit.
*.exe

# A file is written beside its destination and then renamed onto it, so that a
# reader never sees half of one. The temp is removed when the write fails, but a
# process killed outright never gets to remove it — and every commit here sweeps
# the whole tree, so without this one of them lands in the repo. One already did.
*.tmp.[0-9]*

# What one reading of the history turned up on one day. Rewritten whole every
# time the command behind it is run again, and nothing in the repo reads either
# of them back.
#
# Named one at a time rather than as the folder holding them. The folder also
# holds the record of which functions each red gate named, and that one is
# committed on purpose — judging a commit costs minutes and the answer is the
# same for all ten of us, so one of us paying it is all of us knowing. Ignoring
# the folder would have thrown that away and had everybody pay it again.
#
# Only the small one. Its neighbour is 401 KB, and see below for why that
# matters more than its size.
/data/found/findings/git_history_folder_bytes.json

# Ignored on 2026-09-03, together with the drop that is its other half, in one
# sitting with force-push allowed on both remotes. The trap below is why it took
# two attempts, and is still the thing to know about before adding any large
# generated folder to this file.
#
# /public/dev/ was ignored and untracked on 2026-08-15 — 21.64 MB of packed
# history, 93% of it builds that were superseded within the hour. It was put
# back within the hour, because untracking a large file is only half an
# operation. The other half is taking it out of the history, and between the
# two halves the repo is in a state that stops every deploy:
# git_history_heavy_absent_gate_run goes red on every file over 50 KB that the
# history carries and HEAD no longer has, and a red gate that names paths rather
# than functions cannot be placed against any one app — so qa_app_gates_sorted
# counts it against all of them.
#
# So ignore-and-untrack and drop-from-the-history are ONE change, not two, and
# the second half force-pushes both remotes and needs GitLab's branch protection
# taken off by hand. Do not start it without that in place.
#
# When it is done: the rules go here, git_folder_ignored_tracked_remove stops the
# recording, git_history_paths_drop takes them out of the past, and
# git_history_heavy_absent_baseline_write shrinks the ratchet afterwards.
#
# /public/latest/ was held back from the line above on 2026-09-03 and added to it
# the same day, once the question it was waiting on had an answer. It is a deploy
# target, so the worry was that ignoring it would stop the sending. It does not:
# qa_app_commit_deploy builds the app out of the commit it was given and puts the
# pieces it made into this folder, and only then sends. The folder is written by
# the build, not read out of the commit, so tracking it never fed the sending
# anything.
#
# What tracking it did feed was this gate. Every rebuild that renames a chunk
# leaves the old name behind, and each old name over 50 KB is another offender -
# which is how public/745.reply_local.js turned up an hour after eight others had
# just been taken out. That is the whole reason this had to be done twice.
#
# The cost, so it is written down rather than discovered: a FRESH CLONE has an
# empty latest, and the sending puts out the whole folder rather than one app, so
# a deploy from a fresh clone would push a site missing every other app. On the
# machine that does the deploying the files are on disk and nothing changes. If
# it ever does happen, firebase_prod_app_public_live_restore is the way back.
#
# Both moved out of public/ and under web/ on 2026-09-03, the same day, so that
# the folder people are served from holds only what is meant to be public. While
# they were inside it, the working stage was kept off the live site by one line in
# the sending settings naming it, and the checked stage was not named at all and
# so was sent in full - every app's unapproved build, public. Beside it rather
# than inside it, there is nothing to name and nothing to forget to name.
#
# The paths below are the folders' names today. Neither was ever in the history
# under either name, so this move needed no rewrite; the two rules above it are
# kept as written because that is where the files used to be, and a rule naming a
# folder that no longer exists costs nothing while it stops an old checkout of
# this file from recording them again.
/public/dev/
/public/latest/
/web/dev/
/web/latest/

# Python's compiled bytecode for the bash guard. It is generated from the .py
# files beside it and rewritten whenever python decides the cache is stale, so
# tracking it means the tree goes dirty on its own. The QA snapshot checks a
# commit out into its own folder, and a dirty tree there aborts the checkout —
# so every deploy of every app was refused, for a file nobody wrote.
__pycache__/